# OPiO Yield Monitor — Bench Validation Log
# Fast finger-wave through IR beam (first successful optical detection)
# ============================================================================
# Date:            26/08/2026
# Firmware:        Phase 1B (paddle-aware occlusion detection)
# ESP32 board:     AITRIP 30-pin USB-C ESP-WROOM-32 (CP2102)
# Emitter:         CHANZON 940nm IR LED @ 38 kHz PWM on GPIO 25
# Receiver:        Vishay TSOP4838 on GPIO 26
# LED-TSOP gap:    ~3 inches, dome-to-dome, direct line of sight
# Occluder:        Human finger, waved rapidly through the beam
# Wiring:          Direct Dupont F-F jumpers, no breadboard
#
# Historical note:
#   This was the FIRST successful test with real optical hardware after
#   switching from the breadboard build to direct Dupont-jumper connections.
#   The moment the TSOP was wired, beam_state went to CLEAR — retroactively
#   proving that the emitter (LED side) had been working correctly all along
#   during breadboard debugging. The issue was breadboard column continuity,
#   not the LED or resistor.
#
# What the algorithm learned across this segment:
#   - Fast finger waves at up to ~12.7 events/sec
#   - baseline_us settled at ~1,400 µs (finger is small, moves fast)
#   - period_us tracked variations as the finger rate changed
#   - Signal-to-noise for anomaly detection: ~48× (baseline 1.4 ms vs
#     an observed 68 ms wide event) — massive margin for detecting
#     loaded vs unloaded paddles in the field
# ============================================================================

